Title: "Functions"
Id: "40c5a61d-19e7-4856-8fb0-57d09ac6a6ed"


Text: "A function is the mathematical embodiment of a rule that assigns to each
       input exactly one output. Having built sets and their Cartesian products,
       we can now say precisely what a function is: a way of associating with
       every element of one set a unique element of another. Functions, and the
       binary operations that are a special case of them, are the tools with
       which the algebraic structures of the next chapter are described."
Id: "37f9c062-435f-4310-b511-2d2d59281683"


SectionTitle: "Fundamental Function Types"
Id: "73c7927b-afab-4dde-b664-57f27c4b54db"


[\function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Declares: f(x__) ::= y_
when: A, B is \set
specifies:
. x__ "in" A
. y_ "in" B
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then:
  . existsUnique: y "in" B
    suchThat: f(x) = y
Documented:
. called: "function@[A]{ on $A?$}@[B]{ to $B?}$"
. written: "@[A, B]{f? \: : \: A? \rightarrow B?}"
. description: "A function $f : A \rightarrow B$ assigns to each element $x$ of
                the domain $A$ exactly one element $f(x)$ of the codomain $B$.
                The defining requirement is existence and uniqueness of the
                output: for every $x \in A$ there is one and only one $y \in B$
                with $f(x) = y$."
Id: "1e0cb28d-bc8f-42db-bcfa-86a215e88c7a"


Text: "Functions can be classified by how they map inputs to outputs. An
       injective function maps distinct inputs to distinct outputs, a surjective
       function covers every element of its codomain, and a bijection does both."
Id: "252ce10f-e09f-4133-9110-e59fe497ce9c"


[\(injective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f(x__) ::= y_
satisfies:
. forAll: x1, x2 "in" A
  then:
  . if: f(x1) = f(x2)
    then: x1 = x2
Documented:
. adjective:
  . "injective"
  . "one-to-one"
. description: "An injective (one-to-one) function never sends two distinct
                domain elements to the same codomain element."
Id: "c33dd972-b742-4aed-8bbc-797ecdb2b5af"


[\(surjective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f(x__) ::= y_
satisfies:
. forAll: y "in" B
  then:
  . exists: x "in" A
    suchThat: f(x) = y
Documented:
. adjective:
  . "surjective"
  . "onto"
. description: "A surjective (onto) function hits every element of its codomain."
Id: "be68a628-8ca8-49c8-8780-bd7aa7401ef4"


[\(bijective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f
specifies: f is \(injective, surjective)::[[f]]
Documented:
. adjective: "bijective"
. description: "A bijective function is both injective and surjective,
                establishing a one-to-one correspondence between its domain and
                codomain."
Id: "554dad1c-22e4-47ae-9d4f-b0fb088de488"


SectionTitle: "Identity, Composition, and Inverses"
Id: "63269ec1-05b2-4389-8448-8596fd9f1c08"


Text: "The simplest function on any set is the identity function, which leaves
       every element fixed."
Id: "a698fee3-f3a3-4fc8-bb89-3d83f72b6bc9"


[\identity.function:on{A}]
Defines: f is \function:on{A}:to{A}
when: A is \set
expresses: f(x__) := x__
Documented:
. called: "identity function on $A?$"
. written: "\text{id}_{A?}"
. description: "The identity function $\text{id}_A$ maps every element of $A$ to
                itself: $\text{id}_A(x) = x$."
Id: "be488ff3-1570-44a8-8be0-2bc9cbcdd791"


Text: "When the codomain of one function matches the domain of another, they can
       be composed into a single pipeline."
Id: "d6e88a37-b891-428d-bb2d-080654f19c81"


[g(y__) \.function.compose./ f(x__)]
Defines: h(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{C}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. g is \function:on{B}:to{C?}
expresses: h(x__) := g(f(x__))
Documented:
. called: "composition of $g?$ and $f?$"
. written: "g? \circ f?"
. description: "The composition $g \circ f : A \rightarrow C$ applies $f$ first
                and then $g$, so $(g \circ f)(x) = g(f(x))$."
Id: "a5c4b516-227b-4893-aa18-5f04c1f005a4"


Text: "Every bijection admits a unique two-sided inverse function that unwinds
       its action."
Id: "5f0e98c0-5fb7-4b69-9811-55266a75deea"


[\inverse.function:of{f(x__)}]
Defines: g(y__) is \function:on{B}:to{A}
when: f is \(bijective)::function:on{A?}:to{B?}
expresses:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then: g(f(x)) = x
. forAll: y "in" B
  then: f(g(y)) = y
Documented:
. called: "inverse function of $f?$"
. written: "f+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse function $f^{-1} : B \rightarrow A$ of a bijection
                $f$ reverses the mapping, sending each output back to its unique
                source input."
Id: "894fbbc8-5e3d-4fb0-b8c7-124bc834ebcf"


Text: "A function can also be applied to subsets of its domain to yield the
       forward image, or pulled back from subsets of its codomain to yield the
       preimage."
Id: "14fa61b4-4f94-4156-aaf3-cefefc0af688"


[\function.image:of{f(x__)}:on{X}]
Defines: Y := \set@{f(x__) : x__ "in" X}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. X \:subset:/ A
Documented:
. called: "image of $X?$ under $f?$"
. written: "f?[X?]"
. description: "The direct image $f[X]$ is the set of all outputs obtained by
                evaluating $f$ on elements of $X$."
Id: "70ff3a00-b34b-4e76-9bbe-5f3325a59f55"


[\function.preimage:of{f(x__)}:on{Y}]
Defines: X := \set@{x__ : x__ "in" A | f(x__) "in"? Y}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. Y \:subset:/ B
Documented:
. called: "preimage of $Y?$ under $f?$"
. written: "f+?^{-1}[Y?]"
. description: "The preimage (inverse image) $f^{-1}[Y]$ is the set of domain
                elements mapped by $f$ into $Y$."
Id: "d2bd7f7c-06f3-4aff-a5e1-3399e5392f20"


SectionTitle: "Operations and Restrictions"
Id: "e2677e08-e77f-4228-8afc-5280b38b5241"


Text: "A unary operation is a function from a set to itself, transforming
       individual elements."
Id: "4e45bf5d-1f34-4700-8d12-3a34c1eee921"


[\unary.operation:on{X}]
Declares: u(x__) is \function:on{X}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "unary operation on $X?$"
. description: "A unary operation on $X$ is an endofunction from $X$ to $X$."
Id: "3e224a1e-0519-4e11-b080-e748b38de02a"


Text: "A binary operation is a function of two arguments that returns a value in
       the same set. Addition and multiplication are the familiar examples, and
       operations of this kind are exactly what an algebraic structure equips a
       set with."
Id: "dd492fce-0357-4b6a-810f-40764b5245f9"


[\binary.operation:on{X}]
Declares: x_ * y_ is \function:on{X \.set.cross./ X}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "binary operation on $X?$"
. description: "A binary operation on $X$ takes two elements of $X$ and returns
                an element of $X$. Formally it is a function from $X \times X$
                to $X$, written in infix form as $x * y$."
Id: "82c8948a-c30d-464b-8ab0-1c7bdb2ed770"


Text: "Two properties of a binary operation recur constantly. An operation is
       *associative* when the way its arguments are grouped does not matter, and
       *commutative* when their order does not matter. We record both as
       refinements of a binary operation."
Id: "ded59881-7811-4fef-a95c-77f9801814e2"


[\(associative)::binary.operation:on{X}]
Refines: x_ * y_
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b, c "in" X
  then: (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
Documented:
. adjective: "associative"
Id: "e23d5d7d-eed8-4efc-bdda-d316af89684e"


[\(commutative)::binary.operation:on{X}]
Refines: x_ * y_
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  then: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. adjective: "commutative"
Id: "fc0223f8-7053-44ae-a251-94b6c35bc31d"


Text: "A function may be cut down to a smaller domain simply by ignoring the
       inputs outside it. This *restriction* agrees with the original function
       wherever both are defined, and is used later to view an operation on a
       set as an operation on a subset."
Id: "fc9215b0-5d36-4ee6-b3d6-e0007b8e39f5"


[\restriction:of{g(x__)}:on{X}]
Defines: f is \function:on{X}:to{B}
when:
. g is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. X \:subset:/ A
expresses: f(x__) := g(x__)
Documented:
. called: "restriction of $g?$ on $X?$"
. written: "g?\big |_{X?}"
. description: "The restriction $g|_X$ of a function $g : A \rightarrow B$ to a
                subset $X$ of $A$ is the function on $X$ that takes each input
                to the same value $g$ does. It keeps the rule and shrinks the
                domain."
Id: "ea7eab01-792c-427f-91dc-a75f43513b38"


SectionTitle: "Fibers and Factorization"
Id: "f8667ec9-767f-4c75-87a7-07d9ec2fb533"


[\fiber:of{f(x__)}:over{y}]
Defines: X := \set@{x_ : x_ "in" A | f(x_) = y}
when:
. f is \function:on{A?}:to{B?}
. y "in" B
Documented:
. called: "fiber of $f?$ over $y?$"
. written: "f+?^{-1}[\{y?\}]"
. description: "The fiber over $y$ is the set of all domain elements mapped to
                $y$."
Id: "e10a2ceb-8649-480a-9ab4-651ca4345f74"


TextTheorem: "Every function $f:A\to B$ has a canonical factorization
              $A\twoheadrightarrow A/{\sim}\hookrightarrow B$, where $x\sim x'$
              exactly when $f(x)=f(x')$. The first map sends an element to its
              fiber class, and the second sends that class to its common value.
              This separates the identifications made by $f$ from the inclusion
              of its image in the codomain."
Id: "cff9bfde-5ee9-4942-97a5-50b99637c887"


TextTheorem: "A function has a left inverse if and only if it is injective,
              provided a value can be selected outside its image when needed. A
              function has a right inverse if and only if it is surjective; the
              assertion that every surjection has a right inverse is equivalent
              to the axiom of choice. A two-sided inverse exists exactly for
              bijections and is then unique."
Id: "b0b5de59-0d45-45ea-ac3f-6abe4b3e8af1"


TextTheorem: "Direct images preserve arbitrary unions, while inverse images
              preserve arbitrary unions, arbitrary intersections, and
              complements relative to the domain and codomain. Direct images
              preserve intersections only as an inclusion in general; equality
              holds when the function is injective."
Id: "f143d5e8-5f5b-4208-a0b0-f8720288e434"
